(Opens Remote Desktop settings in Windows)

Start ms-settings:remotedesktop

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

(Checks the status of the WinRM service, i.e., Windows Remote Management)

Get-Service WinRM

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

(Stops the WinRM service)

Stop-Service WinRM

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

(Disables automatic startup of WinRM at system boot)

Set-Service WinRM -StartupType Disabled

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

(Shows whether WinRM has active listeners, i.e., configurations listening for remote connections) (if the service is disabled, you will not be able to check for listeners.)

winrm enumerate winrm/config/listener

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

click Start, type regedit Navigate to the following branch:
Find: PortNumber (decimal value) and change it to another, e.g., 48221

HKLM\System\CurrentControlSet\Control\Terminal Server\WinStations\RDP-Tcp

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Start → Settings → System → Remote Desktop:

Check the option "Require devices to use Network Level Authentication to connect"

Get-ItemProperty "HKLM:\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Control\Terminal Server\WinStations\RDP-Tcp" -Name UserAuthentication

Result 1 = NLA enabled.

Enabling via PowerShell:

Set-ItemProperty "HKLM:\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Control\Terminal Server\WinStations\RDP-Tcp" -Name UserAuthentication -Value 1

